    "field": "Ocean circulation and climate change",
    "problem": "Climate models predict that ocean circulation will weaken with global warming, but this contradicts paleoclimate evidence from the last ice age showing that circulation strengthened as the climate warmed, suggesting a fundamental flaw in the models' representation of wind and buoyancy forces.",
    "method": "The paper proposes that warming from increased CO2 strengthens mid-latitude westerly winds by altering atmospheric temperature gradients, which in turn enhances ocean overturning circulation, contrary to climate models that predict a weakening due to surface warming and freshening.",
    "task": "The task is to resolve the contradiction between climate models predicting a weakening ocean circulation and paleoclimate evidence suggesting it strengthened during past warming by re-examining the role of wind forcing on the overturning circulation.",
